KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp11 --unwind 8
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ padded to the field width: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
Meaningful only with the MSVC STL (Windows); elsewhere the #error makes it
fail, which is why it is not CORE: the Linux jobs run every CORE test.
The time-outs had two causes: setw, defined in the runtime, had no model,
so inserting the manipulator called every function of a compatible type
through its unknown function pointer (cpp11_msvc_runtime_manipulator_models);
and num_put formats with sprintf_s, whose UCRT function
__stdio_common_vsprintf_s had no model.  Both are modelled now.  With the
MSVC 14.51 headers preprocessed by cl and --winx64 on Linux (the C library
models compiled from the Linux headers there, so the probe defined
__stdio_common_vsprintf_s as the model does), `ss << setw(4) << 7' still
needs more than 10 GB, for two reasons.  First, Visual Studio's va_arg
reads the value 7 as the bytes of the pointer va_start stores it in,
`(bv)(void *)7', which the simplifier does not fold: the 22 digit steps
of the vsnprintf model divide a symbolic 64-bit value.  Folding the cast
(to `(bv)(size_t)7') makes the program verify in 10 s with
--no-malloc-may-fail, but breaks the constant propagation of pointers
copied by memcpy (cbmc-library/memcpy/constant-propagation_01.desc),
whose bytes then no longer reassemble into the pointer.  Second, without
--no-malloc-may-fail (the default, as unwinding assertions are on) each
locale name the runtime copies with malloc (_Yarn) may fail to be
copied, and the alternatives make the string sizes that follow
symbolic.
